Scope: manifest .swarm/manifests/T14.2.files lists only
web/templates/components/whatif/tax-optimizer.html and
internal/handlers/whatif/tax_optimizer_render_test.go. `git status --porcelain`
before and after my probes is identical (verified below) — no other in-flight
files (classifier/*, dataloader/*, transaction.go, ledger.tsv) were touched by
me or attributed to T14.

Checks that PASSED (no defect found):
1. `go build ./...` — clean, no output/errors.
2. `go test ./...` — all packages pass, including
   internal/handlers/whatif and internal/templates.
3. Zero-rendering contract (tax_optimizer.go ~214-218: "the UI renders '—' for
   zero") is honored in all four places I checked: headline peak-marginal,
   headline Roth-converted, body Peak Rate cell, body Roth Conv cell — all use
   `eq $x 0.0` guards to emit `&mdash;` instead of "0%"/"$0.00".
4. Colspan: template hard-codes 9 (MC) / 7 (non-MC) at line 112, matching the
   actual <th> count; TestWhatIfTaxOptimizer_ColspanMatchesColumnCount asserts
   colspan against the *parsed* <th> count in both modes and passes.
5. Nested-tbody handling: the shared fixture's Top[0] ("best") candidate has
   PerYearConversions set, so every test in this file that calls
   renderTaxOptimizer/theadOf/tbodyOf DOES exercise a real nested
   <thead>/<tbody> inside the outer <tbody>. The tbodyOf LastIndex("</tbody>")
   claim is therefore not merely theoretical — I confirmed via the mutation
   below that content in the outer tbody after the nested table (the zero
   candidate's row) is included in what tbodyOf returns.
6. Roth Conv tooltip text (checked directly): "in nominal (not
   inflation-adjusted) dollars" — does not contain the phrase "today's
   dollars"; matches the claim.
7. Mutation "unconditional dash for Peak Rate cell" (replaced the
   eq-guarded body expression with bare `&mdash;`): caught —
   TestWhatIfTaxOptimizer_RendersPeakRateAndRothConverted fails as expected
   (tbody no longer contains "40%"). File restored, sha256 verified back to
   eda62ab087e9b2094dea9db51bf2c453c697db2f1304c4ddad81a681feae07a0 both before
   and after.

Checks that FAILED (real defect found):
8. Column-swap mutation: swapped the inner expressions of the "Peak Rate" <td>
   (line 99) and "Roth Conv" <td> (line 100) only — headers, colspan, and
   everything else untouched, so the values now render under the WRONG
   column headers (PeakMarginalBracket's "40%" appears under the "Roth Conv"
   header; TotalRothConverted's "$123,456.00" appears under the "Peak Rate"
   header). Ran `go test ./internal/handlers/whatif/... -run TaxOptimizer -v`:
   ALL FOUR tests still PASS. Restored the file afterward and reverified
   sha256sum matches the pre-mutation hash
   (eda62ab087e9b2094dea9db51bf2c453c697db2f1304c4ddad81a681feae07a0).

   Root cause: every assertion in
   TestWhatIfTaxOptimizer_RendersPeakRateAndRothConverted and
   TestWhatIfTaxOptimizer_ZeroRendersAsDash checks only that a value/token is
   *present somewhere* in the scoped tbody/row (strings.Contains), never that
   it appears inside the specific <td> that sits under the "Peak Rate" or
   "Roth Conv" <th>. The task description's claim that the tests verify these
   two new columns "as two table columns after Lifetime Tax" overstates what
   is actually asserted: presence in the tbody is checked, but
   column-identity/position is not. A real regression that swaps which field
   renders in which column — including one that would visibly mislead a user
   about their peak tax bracket vs. their Roth conversion total — would ship
   silently through this test suite.

This is a genuine, reproducible test-coverage gap, not a nitpick: it directly
undermines the accepted claim that the new render test "isolates the zero
candidate's own row" and "counts real <th>s against the parsed colspan" to
guard column integrity — column *identity* (which value lands under which
header) is exactly the kind of regression a reviewer would expect this test
file to catch, and it does not.

git status --porcelain identical before/after my session (confirmed):
same file list as manifest baseline, no stash/reset/checkout/restore/clean/
add/commit/worktree commands were run — only cp (backup/restore) and Read/
python3 in-place edits on the single template file, restored and
